+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+
+=====================
+Smart Task Distributor
+=====================
+
+STD refines wakeup placement toward idle capacity within existing
+classes. Fair tasks consult a tiny fixed-point predictor; real-time
+tasks accept only idle destinations; deadline tasks respect a laxity
+veto before consulting the predictor; idle tasks remain in place.
+Candidates derive from the allowed mask, preferring a fully idle
+core, then any idle CPU, then a spread fallback.
+
+Predictor
+=========
+
+The predictor maps 64 history entries (recent wake CPUs as u8 values
+beside a u8 cursor per task, reset at fork) through 16 hidden s32
+units with ReLU into one s32 verdict weighed against
+``std_nn_thresh``. Deterministic s8 coefficients hold zero mean with
+s32 accumulation built from integer addition and multiplication in
+preallocated non-sleeping wakeup context. ``std_nn_version`` reads
+const 1; ``std_nn_thresh`` starts at 0, adjustable through sysfs and
+the ``std_nn_thresh=`` boot parameter.
+
+Counters
+========
+
+Per-CPU ``fastpath``, ``steered``, ``fallback``, ``nn_keep``,
+``nn_migrate`` and ``split_core`` plus const ``nn_version`` are exposed
+via the read-only ``kernel.std_stats`` vertical 2-column metric|value
+table with SI-1000 scaling. ``kernel.std_running`` (0/1) and
+``kernel.std_nn_thresh`` (s32) are read-write.
+
+Tracing
+=======
+
+Steering stays silent on wakeup. Observe placement through existing
+scheduler tracepoints alongside the STD table::
+
+  cat /proc/sys/kernel/std_stats
+  sysctl kernel.std_running kernel.std_nn_thresh
+  trace-cmd record -e sched_migrate_task -e sched_wakeup -e sched_switch
+  trace-cmd report | head
+  perf stat -e sched:sched_migrate_task taskset -c 0 sleep 0
+
+A ``std_stats`` row set of fastpath/steered/fallback/nn_keep/nn_migrate/
+split_core/nn_version in order with ``| metric | value |`` header and
+``+---+`` borders indicates a healthy STD boot. Cross-check
+``nn_keep``/``nn_migrate`` against ``fastpath``/``steered`` to see
+predictor influence, and ``split_core`` to see whole-core misses.
